## User question:
"During a recent BGP peering session audit on our Nokia 7750 routers, we noticed that some prefixes are being advertised with redundant path attributes—how can I enforce strict single-path advertisement per prefix to simplify route selection and reduce unnecessary overhead?"
---
## Proposed answer:
To enforce strict single-path advertisement per prefix and eliminate redundant path attributes in your BGP peering sessions, use these configuration commands:

```
configure router bgp
    no backup-path
    no aggregate-used-paths
exit

configure router bgp group <group-name>
    min-route-advertisement 30
    neighbor <neighbor-ip>
        no advertise-ldp-prefix
    exit
exit
```

For additional control over prefix propagation, consider adding prefix limits:
```
configure router bgp group <group-name> neighbor <neighbor-ip>
    prefix-limit ipv4 <max-routes> threshold 75 idle-timeout 15
exit
```

These commands will:
1. Disable backup path calculations
2. Prevent aggregation of multiple path attributes
3. Maintain standard route advertisement intervals
4. Disable LDP prefix advertisements
5. Optionally enforce prefix limits with automatic recovery
