Here's the CLI configuration needed to ensure your Nokia routers propagate active /32 LDP FEC routes via BGP while maintaining RFC 3107 label distribution:

```cli
# Enable BGP extended next-hop encoding for labeled IPv4 routes
configure router bgp
    extended-nh-encoding label-ipv4
exit

# Configure LDP as the preferred tunneling mechanism for next-hop resolution
configure router bgp next-hop-resolution labeled-routes transport-tunnel family ipv4
    resolution-filter ldp
    no enforce-strict-tunnel-tagging
exit

# Enable advertisement of LDP prefixes for specific BGP neighbors
configure router bgp group <GROUP_NAME> neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP>
    family ipv4 labeled-unicast
    advertise-ldp-prefix
    import <POLICY_NAME_TO_ALLOW_LDP_FECS>
exit
```

Key elements in this configuration:
1. The `extended-nh-encoding` enables RFC 3107 label distribution capability
2. LDP is set as the resolution filter for IPv4 labeled routes
3. `advertise-ldp-prefix` ensures /32 LDP FECs are advertised even when BGP paths exist
4. An import policy controls which LDP FECs get propagated (replace `<POLICY_NAME_TO_ALLOW_LDP_FECS>` with your actual policy)
