## Proposed answer:
To enforce TCP MD5 authentication for your BGP peering sessions using the existing keychain *BGP_SECURE_KEYS*, apply the following CLI configuration at the appropriate hierarchy levels (global BGP, group, or neighbor):

```cli
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        auth-keychain "BGP_SECURE_KEYS"
```

or for a specific neighbor:

```cli
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        neighbor <ip-address>
            auth-keychain "BGP_SECURE_KEYS"
```

Replace `<group-name>` and `<ip-address>` with your actual BGP group and neighbor details. This ensures all BGP sessions under the specified scope will use the keychain for TCP MD5 authentication while maintaining existing neighbor relationships.
