## User question:
"During a migration to a new peering architecture, we need to ensure our Nokia routers strictly restrict BGP session establishment to legacy 2-byte ASN ranges—what’s the optimal method to implement this constraint without disrupting existing IPv4/IPv6 peerings?"
---
## Proposed answer:
To strictly restrict BGP session establishment to legacy 2-byte ASN ranges while preserving existing IPv4/IPv6 peerings, use the following CLI configuration:

```
configure router bgp
    disable-4byte-asn
exit
```

This command globally disables 4-byte ASN support, ensuring the router only accepts sessions from peers using 2-byte ASNs (1-65535). Existing IPv4/IPv6 peerings with 2-byte ASNs will remain unaffected.

For more granular control at the group or neighbor level, apply the same command under specific groups or neighbors:

```
configure router bgp group <group-name>
    disable-4byte-asn
exit
```

or

```
configure router bgp group <group-name> neighbor <ip-address>
    disable-4byte-asn
exit
