## User question:
"During a planned migration to dual-stack infrastructure, we need to ensure our legacy Nokia routers only participate in IPv4 BGP peering without inadvertently advertising or accepting any extended capabilities—what’s the most reliable method to harden these sessions for strict IPv4 route exchange?"
---
## Proposed answer:
To harden your Nokia router's BGP sessions for strict IPv4 route exchange during the migration, use the following configuration:

```
configure router bgp
    disable-capability-negotiation
    no extended-nh-encoding
exit
```

For each BGP group or neighbor where you want to enforce IPv4-only exchange:
```
configure router bgp group <group-name>
    disable-capability-negotiation
    no extended-nh-encoding
exit

configure router bgp group <group-name> neighbor <ip-address>
    disable-capability-negotiation
    no extended-nh-encoding
exit
```

These commands will:
1. Disable all BGP capability negotiations (preventing extended capabilities)
2. Explicitly remove any extended next-hop encoding capabilities
3. Force the router to operate in strict IPv4-only mode for BGP route exchange
