## User question:
During a recent BGP peering session with multiple client routers in a service provider environment, we observed route reflection loops affecting traffic paths within a specific customer group—what configuration adjustments should be implemented on our Nokia routers to ensure these prefixes aren't inadvertently propagated back to members of the same peer cluster?
---
## Proposed answer:
To prevent route reflection loops within a specific customer group in your BGP peering setup, implement the following configuration adjustments:

```
configure router bgp
    group <customer-group-name>
        disable-client-reflect
        loop-detect discard-route
exit
```

For additional protection against route propagation issues, consider applying these settings to individual neighbors within the group if needed:

```
configure router bgp
    group <customer-group-name>
        neighbor <neighbor-ip>
            disable-client-reflect
            loop-detect discard-route
        exit
    exit
exit
```

These commands will:
1. Disable route reflection to clients within the specified group
2. Implement loop detection that discards routes containing your own AS number
3. Apply these protections either at the group level or per-neighbor basis as required
