Here are the CLI configuration commands needed to extend your existing BGP session to support dual-stack with labeled IPv6 unicast and EVPN NLRI while maintaining backward compatibility:

```
# Configure extended next-hop encoding capabilities
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        neighbor <ipv6-peer-address>
            extended-nh-encoding label-ipv4 vpn-ipv4
            family ipv4 ipv6 evpn
            advertise-ipv6-next-hops vpn-ipv6 label-ipv6 vpn-ipv4 label-ipv4
exit

# Configure next-hop resolution for labeled routes
configure router bgp
    next-hop-resolution
        labeled-routes
            transport-tunnel
                family label-ipv6
                    resolution-filter bgp
                exit
                family label-ipv4
                    resolution-filter bgp
                exit
            exit
        exit
        shortcut-tunnel
            family ipv6
                resolution-filter bgp
            exit
            family ipv4
                resolution-filter bgp
            exit
        exit
    exit
exit
```

Key actions performed:
1. Enabled extended next-hop encoding for both IPv4 and VPN-IPv4 routes
2. Added IPv6 and EVPN address families to the BGP session
3. Configured advertisement of IPv6 next-hops for all relevant route types
4. Set up next-hop resolution for both IPv4 and IPv6 labeled routes
5. Maintained backward compatibility by keeping IPv4 support while adding IPv6 capabilities

The configuration maintains your existing IPv4 BGP peering while adding full dual-stack support with labeled IPv6 unicast and EVPN capabilities.
