## User question:
"During a recent MPLS VPN deployment on our Nokia routers, we noticed that certain VPN-IPv4 and VPN-IPv6 routes aren't being properly processed despite correct route distribution—what's the proper procedure for defining next-hop resolution parameters when these routes haven't been associated with any VPRN instances?"
---
## Proposed answer:
To properly define next-hop resolution parameters for VPN-IPv4 and VPN-IPv6 routes not associated with any VPRN instances, use the following configuration:

```cli
# Enable RR VPN forwarding to process all VPN-IP routes
configure router bgp
    enable-rr-vpn-forwarding
exit

# Configure BGP extended next-hop encoding capabilities
configure router bgp 
    extended-nh-encoding vpn-ipv4
    extended-nh-encoding vpn-ipv6
exit

# Set up next-hop resolution for unassociated VPN routes
configure router bgp next-hop-resolution labeled-routes transport-tunnel
    family vpn
        resolution-filter
            ldp
            rsvp
            sr-isis
            sr-ospf
        exit
    exit
exit

# Optionally configure add-paths if needed (example with 3 paths)
configure router bgp add-paths
    vpn-ipv4 send 3 receive
    vpn-ipv6 send 3 receive
exit
```

This configuration:
1. Enables processing of all VPN-IP routes regardless of route targets
2. Advertises the capability to handle VPN-IPv4/VPN-IPv6 next hops
3. Configures next-hop resolution for unimported VPN routes using LDP, RSVP, and Segment Routing tunnels
4. Optionally enables multipath support for VPN route
