## User question:
"During a maintenance window where we're implementing BGP long-lived graceful restart across our Nokia routers, I need to ensure that a locally defined restart timer of 300 seconds takes precedence over any values received from peers—what's the proper configuration approach to enforce this override?"
---
## Proposed answer:
To enforce a locally defined restart timer of 300 seconds that takes precedence over peer-advertised values during BGP long-lived graceful restart, use the following CLI configuration:

```
configure router bgp graceful-restart long-lived
    helper-override-restart-time 300
exit
```

This command will override any restart-time values received from peers with your locally configured value of 300 seconds for all AFI/SAFIs included in the peer's GR capability.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
helper-override-restart-time <seconds>
no helper-override-restart-time
FuncDef:
This command overrides the restart-time advertised by a peer (in its GR capability) with a locally-configured value. This override applies only to AFI/SAFI that were included in the GR capability of the peer. The restart-time is always zero for AFI/SAFI not included in the GR capability. This command is useful if the local router wants to force LLGR phase to begin after a set time for all protected AFI/SAFI.
ParaDef:
{'Parameters': 'seconds', 'Info': 'The locally-imposed restart time for all AFI/SAFI included in the peer s GR capability. Values-0 to 4095.'}
