To implement extended communities for bandwidth advertisement on your Nokia routers to prioritize certain prefixes, follow these configuration steps:

1. Enable link-bandwidth processing for the BGP group:
```bash
configure router bgp group <group-name> link-bandwidth
```

2. Configure the router to accept link-bandwidth extended communities from EBGP peers for IPv4 routes:
```bash
accept-from-ebgp ipv4
```

3. Optionally, automatically add link-bandwidth extended communities to routes received from directly connected EBGP peers:
```bash
add-to-received-ebgp ipv4
```

4. Allow sending link-bandwidth extended communities to EBGP peers:
```bash
send-to-ebgp ipv4
```

5. Configure aggregation of bandwidth values from multipaths when advertising routes with next-hop-self:
```bash
aggregate-used-paths ipv4
```

For neighbor-specific configurations (if needed), apply these under the neighbor context:
```bash
configure router bgp group <group-name> neighbor <ip-address> link-bandwidth
accept-from-ebgp ipv4
send-to-ebgp ipv4
```

These commands will enable bandwidth-based traffic engineering by propagating and utilizing link-bandwidth extended communities in your BGP routing policies.
