## User question:
During a core network migration where we're transitioning from static routes to BGP peering, what steps should I follow to ensure our Nokia router’s BGP session sources its traffic exclusively from the "core-link1" interface to maintain consistent adjacency with the upstream provider?
---
## Proposed answer:
To configure your Nokia router's BGP session to source traffic exclusively from the "core-link1" interface, use the following CLI commands:

```
configure router
    bgp
        group <peer-group-name>
            neighbor <upstream-provider-ip>
                local-address <core-link1-interface-ip>
                exit
            exit
        exit
    exit
```

Replace `<peer-group-name>` with your BGP peer group name, `<upstream-provider-ip>` with the upstream provider's BGP peer IP address, and `<core-link1-interface-ip>` with the IP address assigned to your "core-link1" interface.

This configuration ensures all BGP traffic to the specified neighbor will originate from the designated "core-link1" interface, maintaining consistent adjacency with your upstream provider.
