---
## Proposed answer:
To ensure seamless BGP route advertisement consistency while retaining the original BGP next-hop attribute for both labeled IPv4 and IPv6 prefixes to a designated peer group, use the following CLI configuration:

```cli
configure router bgp
    group <peer-group-name>
        next-hop-unchanged label-ipv4 label-ipv6
        no next-hop-self
exit
```

If the peers are in the same subnet and you want to preserve third-party next-hops for EBGP peers, add:
```cli
configure router bgp
    group <peer-group-name>
        third-party-nexthop
exit
```

For IPv6 transport sessions where you need to advertise IPv6 next-hops for labeled IPv4/IPv6 prefixes:
```cli
configure router bgp
    group <peer-group-name>
        advertise-ipv6-next-hops label-ipv4 label-ipv6
exit
```

These commands will prevent the router from modifying the next-hop attribute while propagating routes to the specified peer group.
