## User question:
"During a recent core network upgrade involving Nokia routers, we observed BGP session instability due to TCP packet fragmentation along the path; what strategies can we employ to dynamically adjust MTU settings based on ICMP 'Packet Too Big' messages from transit devices to ensure reliable BGP peering?"
---
## Proposed answer:
To address BGP session instability caused by TCP packet fragmentation, enable Path MTU Discovery for your BGP sessions. This will allow the router to dynamically adjust the MTU based on ICMP "Packet Too Big" messages from transit devices:

```
configure router bgp
    path-mtu-discovery
exit
```

For more granular control, you can enable Path MTU Discovery at the group or neighbor level:

```
configure router bgp group <group-name>
    path-mtu-discovery
exit
```

or

```
configure router bgp group <group-name> neighbor <ip-address>
    path-mtu-discovery
exit
```

These commands will configure the router to:
1. Initially use the egress interface MTU for BGP TCP sessions
2. Set the DF (Don't Fragment) bit in packets
3. Dynamically adjust the MTU when receiving ICMP "Packet Too Big" messages
4. Maintain stable BGP peering by avoiding fragmentation issues along the path
