## User question:
"During a core network upgrade, we're implementing stricter route control for our Nokia routers—given that a critical BGP neighbor tends to flood prefixes, what’s the proper way to enforce a hard cap of 5000 IPv6 routes while setting up proactive monitoring (triggering alerts at 80% utilization) and ensuring the session automatically recovers within half an hour if the ceiling is breached?"
---
## Proposed answer:
Here's the CLI configuration to implement strict route control for your BGP neighbor:

```
configure router bgp
    group <your-group-name>
        neighbor <neighbor-IP>
            family ipv6
                prefix-limit ipv6 5000 threshold 80 idle-timeout 30
            exit
        exit
    exit
exit
```

This configuration will:
1. Enforce a hard limit of 5000 IPv6 routes from the neighbor
2. Trigger alerts when 4000 routes (80%) are received
3. Automatically recover the session after 30 minutes if the limit is exceeded

For additional protection, you might want to enable BGP fast reroute for IPv6:

```
configure router bgp
    backup-path ipv6
exit
