To prevent route propagation loops in your BGP peering session, implement outbound route filtering and configure loop detection with the following CLI commands:

```cli
configure router bgp
    group TRANSIT_PROVIDER
        neighbor <NEIGHBOR_IP>
            outbound-route-filtering
                send
                    family ipv4
                    family ipv6
                exit
            exit
            loop-detect discard-route
            import "POLICY_TO_BLOCK_READVERTISEMENT"
exit
```

Replace `<NEIGHBOR_IP>` with your transit provider's BGP peer address and `"POLICY_TO_BLOCK_READVERTISEMENT"` with a policy that filters routes learned from this neighbor. 

The key elements are:
1. Enabling ORF (`outbound-route-filtering`) to exchange filter lists dynamically
2. Setting `loop-detect discard-route` to automatically discard routes containing your own AS
3. Applying an import policy to explicitly block re-advertisement of learned routes

For complete protection, ensure your import policy matches all prefixes received from this neighbor and prevents their re-advertisement.
